- **Source type**: Monograph (book)  
- **URL**: https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/000609034  
- **Query that triggered it**: `"Ring of Nestor" Mycenaean gold ring`  
- **Relevance**: This is Evans’s original study of the “Ring of Nestor” and 
associated gold signet-rings and bead-seals from a sepulchral context at Thisbe,
Boeotia. It is the foundational publication for the identification, description,
discovery context, interpretations, and cultural attribution of the ring.

- **Available bibliographic/summary details (from HathiTrust entry and Nature 
review)**:
  - Full title (as given in a contemporary review):  
    “The Ring of Nestor: a Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral 
Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbe, Boeotia.” [4]
  - Described explicitly as “a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbe, Boeotia” associated with the “Ring of Nestor.” [4]
  - The HathiTrust catalog entry records that it is a printed volume (“Pp. ii + 
75 + 5 plates” given by the Nature review) and attributes the work to Sir Arthur
Evans. [4]

- **Extracted/usable points (for later article-writing)**:
  - The object identified as the “Ring of Nestor” is a gold signet ring, part of
a treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals.
  - The treasure is associated with a sepulchral (funerary) context at Thisbe in
Boeotia.
  - The work is explicitly framed as giving “a glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world,” implying Evans’s interpretive emphasis on Minoan 
religious/afterlife ideas reflected in the ring’s imagery and in related signet 
rings.
  - Evans’s monograph is the main primary report on the find: it includes 
detailed description of the ring, iconography, and context (including plates).

*(Full text is accessible through HathiTrust or library holdings; further 
details like exact dimensions, detailed iconographic motifs, tomb structure, 
etc., would come from the body and plates of Evans’s book.)*

