### Source 1

- **Title**: Rare archaeological finds in Pylos  
- **URL**: 
https://www.archaeology.wiki/blog/2015/10/27/rare-archaeological-finds-pylos  
- **Publisher / Author**: Archaeology Wiki (Archaiologia Online; appears to be 
associated with the Greek Ministry of Culture’s Archaeology & Arts portal, but 
the specific author is not named)  
- **Date**: 27 October 2015  
- **Source type**: Online archaeology news / magazine article  
- **Query triggering this source**: `"Ring of Nestor" gold ring Pylos excavation
discovery history`  
- **Relevance**:  
  High. Discusses the Griffin Warrior tomb discovered near the Palace of Nestor 
at Pylos in 2015 and mentions one of the gold rings (with a Cretan bull-jumping 
scene) found in the tomb. Useful for discovery date, site, basic context of the 
grave and its contents, and association with the University of Cincinnati team.

- **Key content (faithful summary)**:  
  - Reports the discovery of a Bronze Age warrior’s tomb in Pylos, southwestern 
Greece, by an international team led by University of Cincinnati archaeologists.
  - The tomb contained more than 1,400 objects: jewels, weapons, armor, and 
bronze, silver, and gold vessels, described as a rich and rare discovery.  
  - It states that the grave was found near the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at 
Ano Englianos.  
  - The tomb is dated to about 1500 BC.  
  - A gold ring with a Cretan bull‑jumping scene is described as one of four 
solid-gold rings found in the tomb.  
  - Other grave goods include ivory combs, a golden necklace, and numerous seal 
stones with intricate Minoan designs.  
  - The article notes that the discovery was featured in The New York Times 
under the title “A Warrior’s Grave at Pylos, Greece, Could Be a Gateway to 
Civilizations.”

